issues.body:
### What issue are you seeing?

When a sub-agent is spawned, its instructions come only from its parent thread. In `ThreadManagerState::instructions_for_spawn` the parent lookup error is mapped to empty instructions without any log:

https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/326bac6ce0ad9e91fbe5db1d2be5534bed1b50b5/codex-rs/core/src/thread_manager.rs#L2364-L2373

```rust
let inherited = match inherited_thread_id {
    Some(thread_id) => match self.get_thread(thread_id).await {
        Ok(thread) => thread.session.inherited_instructions().await,
        Err(_) => SessionInstructions::default(),
    },
    None => SessionInstructions::default(),
};
if session_source.is_non_root_agent() {
    inherited
} else { ... }
```

`get_thread` returns `ThreadNotFound` when the parent is not in the in-memory registry, and also when it is there but its `session_source.is_internal()` is true (L2156-L2162). For a non-root agent the result is returned as is, so in those cases the sub-agent starts with no user instructions and no AGENTS.md content, and nothing in the logs says why. Root threads are not affected because they get `user_provider` re-attached a few lines below.

### What steps can reproduce the bug?

From reading the code, any spawn where `session_source` is `SubAgent(ThreadSpawn { parent_thread_id, .. })` and `get_thread(parent_thread_id)` fails, for example a sub-agent thread that is started again in a process where its parent is not loaded, or a sub-agent whose parent is an internal session. I have not built an end-to-end reproduction through the CLI; the path above is the only branch that produces the empty instructions.

### What is the expected behavior?

Either the sub-agent falls back to the same sources a root thread uses (the global `user_instructions_provider` plus AGENTS.md), or the fallback is at least logged with the parent thread id so the missing instructions can be diagnosed. The sibling helpers `inherited_multi_agent_version` and `inherited_originator_for_parent_thread` use the same `.ok()` pattern, so the same decision probably applies to them.

### Additional information

Seen at `main` 326bac6. A `tracing::warn!` with the thread id in the `Err` arm would be the smallest change; falling back to `self.user_instructions_provider` for non-root agents would keep the project rules in effect.

### What happened?

`submission.wait()` returns `done` when the model stops with `length` and its only content is `codemode({})`. The submission's `answer` points to that tool call. The transcript has no `pi.tool-result` and no final text. A caller that checks the submission status sees a completed input.

### Steps to reproduce

1. Save the script below as `repro.mjs` in an empty directory and install the listed packages. It uses Pi's `fauxProvider`, `MemoryStorage` and built-in `codemode` tool.
2. Run `node repro.mjs`. The harness makes one model request and returns the unexecuted tool call as the answer.
3. Run `node repro.mjs toolUse`. With the same `{}` arguments, the harness records a tool error, makes a second model request and returns `follow-up answer`.

<details><summary>Install command and standalone script</summary>

```sh
npm init -y
npm install --ignore-scripts @earendil-works/pi-durable@1.6.0 @earendil-works/pi-ai@1.6.0 @earendil-works/pi-codemode@1.6.0 @earendil-works/chord@1.6.0
```

```js
import { BACKGROUND_CONTEXT as ctx } from "@earendil-works/chord/context";
import { createModels, fauxAssistantMessage, fauxProvider, fauxToolCall } from "@earendil-works/pi-ai";
import { CodemodeSandbox } from "@earendil-works/pi-codemode";
import { AssistantEntry, createRegistry, Harness, MemoryStorage, ToolResultEntry } from "@earendil-works/pi-durable";
import { createCodemodeExtension } from "@earendil-works/pi-durable/codemode";

const [stopReason = "length", args = "{}"] = process.argv.slice(2);
const faux = fauxProvider();
faux.setResponses([
  fauxAssistantMessage([fauxToolCall("codemode", JSON.parse(args), { id: "call-1" })], { stopReason }),
  fauxAssistantMessage("follow-up answer"),
]);
const models = createModels();
models.setProvider(faux.provider);
const registry = createRegistry();
registry.install(createCodemodeExtension({ sandbox: new CodemodeSandbox(), models: false }));

const harness = await Harness.open(new MemoryStorage(), { models, registry }, ctx);
try {
  const root = await harness.root(ctx, { agent: { model: { provider: "faux", modelId: "faux-1" } } });
  harness.resume();
  const submission = await root.submit({ type: "input", content: "Run codemode, then reply." }, ctx);
  const settled = await submission.wait(ctx);
  const { items } = await root.entries({}, 100, undefined, ctx);
  const toolResults = items.filter((entry) => ToolResultEntry.is(entry)).map((entry) => entry.model[0]);
  const answer = settled.answer && await root.commit((tx) => tx.entry(AssistantEntry, settled.answer), ctx);
  console.log(JSON.stringify({
    status: settled.status,
    modelCalls: faux.state.callCount,
    answerStopReason: answer?.model[0].stopReason,
    answerContent: answer?.model[0].content,
    toolResults: toolResults.map(({ toolCallId, isError }) => ({ toolCallId, isError })),
  }, null, 2));
} finally {
  await harness.close(ctx);
}
```

</details>

### Expected behavior

Keep the truncated call unexecuted. Give callers a way to distinguish an unanswered input at settlement, or record a truncation error for the tool call so the model can respond.

### Version

`@earendil-works/pi-durable@1.6.0`, Node.js `24.22.1`, macOS arm64. The reproduction ran with network access disabled.

### What do you want to change?

Keep the current branch selected when `SessionManager.branchWithSummary()` fails to write the summary (This may happen for many reasons..). The next message should still continue from the branch that was active before the failed switch.



### Why?

A session can hold several branches in one JSONL file. Each line is a JSON record, and entries use `parentId` to form the tree. The active leaf is the entry Pi uses as the parent for the next append.

The following is an example: `B` is the active leaf and `A` is the earlier entry we want to branch from.

```text
Before the attempt
A ── B (active)

After a successful summary write
A ──┬── B
    └── S (active summary)
```

When the summary write throws, the active leaf should stay at `B`. But now Pi leaves it at `A`, so the next message `C` is saved on a different branch.

```text
Expected after the failed write and next message
A ── B ── C

Actual after the failed write and next message (which is not the expected behavior)
A ──┬── B
    └── C
```

Reproduced on macOS with Pi 1.6.0 from upstream [`main` at `69a6993`](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/commit/69a6993486acee850d14a4a1547daa4a167228ad). The test calls `SessionManager` directly and runs without extensions or provider credentials.

<details>
<summary>Reproduce with Vitest</summary>

1. Create a session backed by a JSONL file in a temporary directory. Append messages `A` and `B`.
2. Move the test JSONL to a backup path and create a directory at the original path. This makes the next file append fail predictably with `EISDIR` on macOS.
3. Call `branchWithSummary(A, "summary")` and confirm it throws. Record the active leaf, then remove the placeholder directory and move the original file back.
4. Append `C` using the **same manager**, then reopen the file to check `C.parentId` on disk. Both the leaf after the failure and the saved parent of `C` should be `B`. On stock Pi, both are `A`.

Save the test as `packages/coding-agent/test/session-manager/branch-summary-repro.test.ts`. From `packages/coding-agent` in a checkout with dependencies prepared, run:

```sh
node ../../node_modules/vitest/vitest.mjs run test/session-manager/branch-summary-repro.test.ts
```

```ts
import { mkdirSync, mkdtempSync, renameSync, rmdirSync, rmSync } from "fs";
import { tmpdir } from "os";
import { join } from "path";
import { expect, it } from "vitest";
import { SessionManager } from "../../src/core/session-manager.ts";

it("keeps the active branch when writing a branch summary fails", () => {
	const dir = mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), "pi-branch-summary-"));
	try {
		const session = SessionManager.create(dir, dir);
		const a = session.appendMessage({ role: "user", content: "A", timestamp: 1 });
		const b = session.appendMessage({ role: "user", content: "B", timestamp: 2 });
		const path = session.getSessionFile()!;
		const backup = join(dir, "session-backup.jsonl");

		renameSync(path, backup);
		mkdirSync(path);
		expect(() => session.branchWithSummary(a, "summary")).toThrow();
		const leafAfterFailure = session.getLeafId();

		rmdirSync(path);
		renameSync(backup, path);
		const c = session.appendMessage({ role: "user", content: "C", timestamp: 3 });
		const savedParent = SessionManager.open(path, dir).getEntry(c)?.parentId;

		expect({ leafAfterFailure, savedParent }).toEqual({ leafAfterFailure: b, savedParent: b });
	} finally {
		rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true });
	}
});
```

</details>

### How? (optional)

`branchWithSummary()` sets the active leaf to `A` before `_appendEntry()` captures the leaf it will restore on failure. The summary already has `A` as its `parentId`. Removing the early assignment lets the append helper preserve `B` if the write fails and select `S` when the write succeeds. I’d like to implement the fix (by myself) and add the corresponding regression test.

### What happened?

With `--tools read,grep`, an extension can switch the session to `read` only. Registering an unrelated tool then restores `grep`, so it is offered to the model again. The unrelated tool is filtered out. The [refresh code](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/9c4652688590abb836b40ebd1e4725ecf9a7c7d1/packages/coding-agent/src/core/agent-session.ts#L3737-L3749) reapplies the entire startup list.

### Steps to reproduce

Save this as `repro.mjs`:

```js
export default function (pi) {
  pi.on("session_start", () => {
    pi.setActiveTools(["read"]);
    console.error("before:", pi.getActiveTools().join(","));
    pi.registerTool({
      name: "unrelated",
      label: "Unrelated",
      description: "Return an empty result",
      parameters: { type: "object", properties: {} },
      execute: async () => ({ content: [], details: {} }),
    });
    console.error("after:", pi.getActiveTools().join(","));
  });
}
```

Run `pi -ne -e ./repro.mjs --mode rpc --no-session --tools read,grep`. At startup it prints `before: read` then `after: read,grep`. The only loaded extension is the fixture. No model request is needed. This focuses on an existing allowlisted tool with one extension, related to #10430.

### Expected behavior

`after: read`. Registering a tool should preserve the session's selection for tools already registered.

I'm happy to implement the fix and open a PR once approved.

### Version

`1.5.1` (source build at `4ea883792`, Windows).

### What happened?

If one `before_agent_start` handler returns `{ systemPrompt }` and a later handler adds to `event.systemPromptOptions.sections`, the added section never reaches the provider. The session transcript still records it, so nothing looks wrong.

In my setup this drops `reply_style` from pi-emphasis and the built-in `mcp_servers` section whenever any string-returning extension (pi-memory, ponytail, pi-better-background-tasks, ...) loads before them.

Cause as far as I can tell: the returned string becomes `forceSystemPrompt` (runner.js `emitBeforeAgentStart`), and `buildSystemPrompt` returns only that string once it is set, so later section edits have no effect.

### Steps to reproduce

`a-string.ts`:
```ts
export default function (pi) {
  pi.on("before_agent_start", (e) => ({ systemPrompt: `${e.systemPrompt}\n\nMARKER_A` }));
}
```

`b-section.ts`:
```ts
export default function (pi) {
  pi.on("before_agent_start", (e) => { (e.systemPromptOptions.sections ??= {}).repro_b = "MARKER_B"; });
  pi.on("before_provider_request", (e) => {
    const s = JSON.stringify(e.payload);
    console.error(`A=${s.includes("MARKER_A")} B=${s.includes("MARKER_B")}`);
  });
}
```

```
pi -ne -ns -nc --no-session -e ./a-string.ts -e ./b-section.ts -p "reply with just: ok"
# A=true B=false
pi -ne -ns -nc --no-session -e ./b-section.ts -e ./a-string.ts -p "reply with just: ok"
# A=true B=true
```

### Expected behavior

Both markers reach the provider regardless of load order, or Pi warns that a section was dropped.

Possibly related: #9932, #10489 (same forced-prompt path).

### Version

1.6.0

In the Inbox, I dismiss **Archive check** and it disappears. I dismiss **Index check** and **Archive check** comes straight back. I never closed the Inbox, and neither job ran again.

## Steps to reproduce

1. Open the Control UI in a disposable Chromium profile. Set up two enabled automations whose last runs failed. I used two yearly command jobs that exit with status 1 and ran each once.
2. Open **Inbox**, select **System**, and fill this site's local storage. I used this in DevTools in the disposable profile.

   ```js
   for (let size = 2 ** 20; size >= 1; size /= 2) {
     try {
       while (true) {
         const previous = localStorage.getItem("inbox-fill") || "";
         localStorage.setItem("inbox-fill", previous + "x".repeat(size));
       }
     } catch (error) {
       if (error.name !== "QuotaExceededError") throw error;
     }
   }
   ```

3. Click the X on the first job, then click the X on the other one.

**Expected.** Both jobs stay dismissed in the open Inbox. If OpenClaw can't remember that after a reload, I'd like it to tell me.

**Actual.** After the second click, only Archive check is visible. Reloading with storage full also brought a dismissed job back. With space available, both dismissals survived a reload. The dismissal handler reads the list from local storage on each click. A failed write is caught, and the UI still uses the new list in memory. [Dismissal handler](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/22255926f9cf4fd7a297d5a6dc3f6abe58c52d97/ui/src/components/sidebar-attention-dismissals.ts#L70-L107)

Tested on macOS with Chromium and a local gateway built from `22255926f9cf`.

![Inbox before and after dismissing Archive check](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/daniel-lxs/screenshots/d03cca34aa7607a5e57e0b086e000cf4fdede187/images/2026/11/0a45fe9e-c176-4312-add5-91f7cd7aab1f.png)

![Archive check is back after dismissing Index check](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/daniel-lxs/screenshots/20569d66ed8fc7fb9494dbba96f5a91d8709567d/images/2026/11/4a81d405-b5e1-45d4-ba84-e46ed1555ae0.png)

