## What Problem This Solves

Two JSON log records with consecutive numeric IDs could look identical to `openclaw path`. Looking up the first ID returned both records, while looking up the second returned no matches.

## User Impact

`path resolve` reports the digits stored in a plain integer ID, and an exact `path find` filter selects the matching record in JSON, JSONC, and JSONL.

## Why This Change Was Made

JavaScript can round distinct large integers to the same number. The parser now retains the original digits for plain decimal integers outside the safe range. Leaf output and exact-match filters use the same text. Numeric ordering and edits still use their existing number semantics.

## Evidence

Ran the built CLI against a two-line JSONL file containing `9007199254740992` and `9007199254740993`. On the earlier build, resolving line 2 printed the first ID, and the two searches matched two records and zero records. With the fix, line 2 reports the second ID and each search returns exactly its own line.

Both regression cases failed on the unmodified base, for JSONC and JSONL. The fixed plugin passed all 512 tests, including lookups of negative IDs. The runtime build, extension and test typechecks, formatting, and lint passed. The optional full-repository change check exits 1 on unused exports. A clean checkout of the same base reports the identical 15 production and 26 full-tree findings, all outside oc-path.

