## What Problem This Solves

`oc-path` parsed every JSON number into a JS double and threw the literal away, so distinct integers past 2^53 collapsed: with

```jsonl
{"id":9007199254740992}
{"id":9007199254740993}
```

`openclaw path resolve 'oc://events.jsonl/L2/id'` reported `9007199254740992`, `path find '[id=9007199254740993]'` matched nothing (exit 1), and `path find '[id=9007199254740992]'` matched both records. Fixes #203589.

## User Impact

Selectors and resolves over JSONL/JSONC files whose IDs are large integer literals now report the digits actually stored on that line, and an exact predicate matches only the record that carries them.

Every other number keeps its previous reading: `1e3` still matches `[id=1000]` and resolves as `1000`, `1.0` still matches `[id=1]` and resolves as `1`.

## Why This Change Was Made

`nodeToJsoncValue` stored `{ kind: "number", value: Number(node.value) }`, and both consumers stringified `value.value` (`jsoncChildFieldText` for line predicates, `jsoncValueToMatch` for resolve output). Both neighbours therefore read as `9007199254740992`.

Number nodes now carry the exact source literal in an optional `raw` field, but **only for plain integer literals outside the safe range** (`|n| > 2^53 - 1`) — exactly the literals a double cannot represent. `jsoncChildFieldText` and `jsoncValueToMatch` prefer `raw` when present and otherwise keep `String(value)`.

That leaves the original uniform kind-dispatched addressing from #81436 (numeric leaves read from their parsed values) intact for every number that round-trips; the source-text override exists only where the parsed value and the literal disagree. `-0`, `1e3`, `1.0` and every other spelling stay on the normalized path, which the new test file pins directly at the parser. Round-tripping/emit is untouched — the parser always retained raw file bytes.

